{
  "name": "cliff-crowd-counterfactual",
  "description": "MUST FAIL -- run it with --expect-fail. The cliff-crowd timeline (so the ROM really is on CliffCrowd) judged against cliff-crowd-reordered's expected values. The assertions the two maps SHARE must still PASS; only the ones the cliff changes may go red, and that split is the proof that the failure is aimed at the cliff and not at the run. Generated by make_cliffs.py --specs.",
  "symbols_from": "cliffs",
  "symbols_from_why": "Every probe here names a STOCK engine global (gMapHeader, gBackupMapLayout, sBackupMapData, gObjectEvents) -- this fixture adds no RAM at all. The key is still declared because the MAP the spec is about exists only in this fork, and a run against the pinned engine would resolve every symbol and assert nonsense.",
  "screenshot": false,
  "timeline": [
    {
      "name": "boot",
      "frames": 900,
      "keys": [
        "B"
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "settle",
      "frames": {
        "random_range": [
          30,
          90
        ]
      },
      "keys": [
        "B"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "probes": [
    {
      "id": "layout_id",
      "why": "gMapHeader.mapLayoutId. Which of the four fixture maps the boot-key selector actually chose. Doc 66's rule: assert that the thing under test happened, or a green run may be a run of something else.",
      "symbol": "gMapHeader",
      "type": "u16",
      "sample": {
        "at_step_start": [
          "settle"
        ],
        "at_step_end": [
          "settle"
        ],
        "at": [
          910,
          925
        ]
      },
      "offset": 18
    },
    {
      "id": "grid_w",
      "why": "gBackupMapLayout.width. InitMapLayoutData sets width and height BEFORE the ceiling test, so these are correct on both sides of the cliff -- which is exactly why the failure is invisible.",
